# =====================================================================
@subtopic Arrays & Strings

[EASY]
Q: What is the index of the first element of an array in Java, C and Python?
A) 0
B) 1
C) -1
D) Depends on the array size
ANSWER: A
EXPLAIN: All three languages use zero-based indexing.

[EASY]
Q: What is the time complexity of accessing the element at index i of an array?
A) O(1)
B) O(log n)
C) O(n)
D) O(i)
ANSWER: A
EXPLAIN: The address is computed directly as base + i x element size.

[EASY]
Q: Array elements are stored in memory:
A) At random locations linked by pointers
B) In contiguous (adjacent) memory locations
C) In a tree structure
D) Only on the disk
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Inserting an element at the beginning of an array of n elements takes:
A) O(1)
B) O(log n)
C) O(n)
D) O(n^2)
ANSWER: C
EXPLAIN: Every existing element must be shifted one position to the right.

[EASY]
Q: In Java, which method returns the number of characters in a String s?
A) s.size()
B) s.length()
C) s.length
D) s.count()
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: Strings use the method length(); arrays use the field length.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] a = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5};
        int s = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < a.length; i += 2) {
            s += a[i];
        }
        System.out.println(s);
    }
}
END
A) 2
B) 9
C) 14
D) 6
ANSWER: A
HINT: The loop starts at index 1 and jumps by 2, so only odd indices are visited.
EXPLAIN: Indices 1 and 3 hold 1 and 1, so s = 2.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What does this Python code print?
CODE python
s = "datastructure"
print(s[2:6], s[-3:])
END
A) tast ure
B) atas ure
C) tast ture
D) tas ure
ANSWER: A
HINT: s[2:6] takes indices 2, 3, 4 and 5; s[-3:] takes the last three characters.
EXPLAIN: Index 2..5 of "datastructure" is "tast"; the last three characters are "ure".

[MEDIUM]
Q: A 2D array int a[4][5] is stored in row-major order starting at address 1000 with 4 bytes per element. What is the address of a[2][3]?
A) 1052
B) 1048
C) 1056
D) 1076
ANSWER: A
HINT: Address = base + (row x number_of_columns + col) x size.
EXPLAIN: 1000 + (2 x 5 + 3) x 4 = 1000 + 52 = 1052.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] a = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        int k = 2;
        int n = a.length;
        int[] r = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            r[(i + k) % n] = a[i];
        }
        for (int x : r) System.out.print(x + " ");
    }
}
END
A) 4 5 1 2 3
B) 3 4 5 1 2
C) 2 3 4 5 1
D) 5 4 3 2 1
ANSWER: A
HINT: Each element moves k positions to the right, wrapping around the end.
EXPLAIN: This is a right rotation by 2: element 1 goes to index 2, 4 goes to index 0, 5 to index 1.

[HARD]
Q: Using Kadane's algorithm, what is the maximum subarray sum of [-2, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1, -5, 4]?
A) 6
B) 7
C) 5
D) 4
ANSWER: A
HINT: Keep a running sum and reset it to the current element whenever the running sum becomes worse than starting fresh.
EXPLAIN: The best subarray is [4, -1, 2, 1] with sum 6.

