@subtopic Linked Lists

[EASY]
Q: Each node of a singly linked list contains:
A) Only data
B) Data and a pointer to the next node
C) Data and pointers to the next and previous nodes
D) Only a pointer
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: What is the time complexity of inserting a node at the head of a singly linked list?
A) O(1)
B) O(log n)
C) O(n)
D) O(n log n)
ANSWER: A
EXPLAIN: Only two pointer assignments are needed, regardless of list length.

[EASY]
Q: Accessing the k-th element of a singly linked list takes:
A) O(1)
B) O(k)
C) O(log k)
D) O(k^2)
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: There is no random access; you must walk k nodes from the head.

[EASY]
Q: In a circular linked list, the last node points to:
A) null
B) Itself
C) The first node
D) The middle node
ANSWER: C

[EASY]
Q: Which advantage does a doubly linked list have over a singly linked list?
A) It uses less memory
B) It can be traversed in both directions
C) It allows O(1) random access
D) It never needs pointers
ANSWER: B

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    static class Node {
        int val; Node next;
        Node(int v, Node n) { val = v; next = n; }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Node head = new Node(1, new Node(2, new Node(3, null)));
        Node prev = null, cur = head;
        while (cur != null) {
            Node nxt = cur.next;
            cur.next = prev;
            prev = cur;
            cur = nxt;
        }
        for (Node p = prev; p != null; p = p.next) System.out.print(p.val + " ");
    }
}
END
A) 1 2 3
B) 3 2 1
C) 3
D) 1
ANSWER: B
HINT: Watch what happens to cur.next in each iteration: it is pointed backwards.
EXPLAIN: This is the classic in-place reversal; prev ends at the old tail.

[MEDIUM]
Q: Two pointers start at the head; slow moves 1 step and fast moves 2 steps at a time. When fast reaches the end of a list with 7 nodes (values 1..7), which node is slow at?
A) 3
B) 4
C) 5
D) 7
ANSWER: B
HINT: The fast pointer covers twice the distance of the slow pointer.
EXPLAIN: Slow visits 1,2,3,4 while fast visits 1,3,5,7 — slow stops at the middle node, 4.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this Python code?
CODE python
class Node:
    def __init__(self, v, nxt=None):
        self.v, self.next = v, nxt

head = Node(10, Node(20, Node(30, Node(40))))
head.next = head.next.next
total, p = 0, head
while p:
    total += p.v
    p = p.next
print(total)
END
A) 100
B) 80
C) 70
D) 60
ANSWER: B
HINT: The second line skips one node. Which one?
EXPLAIN: head.next now points to 30, so the node 20 is removed: 10 + 30 + 40 = 80.

[HARD]
Q: Floyd's cycle-detection algorithm uses a slow and a fast pointer. After they meet inside the cycle, how is the START of the cycle found?
A) Move both pointers one step at a time from the meeting point until they meet again
B) Reset one pointer to the head, then move both one step at a time; they meet at the cycle start
C) Reverse the list from the meeting point
D) Count the nodes in the cycle and divide by two
ANSWER: B
HINT: The distance from the head to the cycle start equals the distance from the meeting point to the cycle start (mod cycle length).
EXPLAIN: If the head-to-start distance is a and the meeting point is b steps into the cycle of length c, then a = c - b (mod c), so pointers moving from head and meeting point meet at the start.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    static class Node {
        int v; Node next;
        Node(int v) { this.v = v; }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Node head = null, tail = null;
        for (int i = 1; i <= 6; i++) {
            Node n = new Node(i);
            if (head == null) head = tail = n; else { tail.next = n; tail = n; }
        }
        Node p = head;
        while (p != null && p.next != null) {
            p.next = p.next.next;
            p = p.next;
        }
        for (Node q = head; q != null; q = q.next) System.out.print(q.v + " ");
    }
}
END
A) 1 3 5
B) 2 4 6
C) 1 2 3
D) 1 3 5 6
ANSWER: A
HINT: Each iteration unlinks the node right after p, then jumps p forward.
EXPLAIN: Nodes 2, 4 and 6 are unlinked in turn, leaving 1 3 5.

