[EASY]
Q: Which data structure follows the LIFO (Last In, First Out) principle?
A) Queue
B) Stack
C) Array
D) Tree
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which data structure follows the FIFO (First In, First Out) principle?
A) Stack
B) Queue
C) Graph
D) Heap
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Removing an element from the top of a stack is called:
A) Push
B) Pop
C) Enqueue
D) Peek
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: Which data structure does the computer use to manage function calls and recursion?
A) Queue
B) Call stack
C) Hash table
D) Graph
ANSWER: B

[EASY]
Q: In a circular queue implemented with an array, the rear index after insertion is updated as:
A) rear = rear + 1
B) rear = (rear + 1) % capacity
C) rear = rear - 1
D) rear = capacity - rear
ANSWER: B
EXPLAIN: The modulo makes the index wrap back to 0 after the last slot.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Deque<Integer> st = new ArrayDeque<>();
        st.push(1);
        st.push(2);
        st.push(3);
        st.pop();
        st.push(4);
        System.out.println(st.pop() + st.peek());
    }
}
END
A) 6
B) 7
C) 5
D) 42
ANSWER: A
HINT: After pop and push, the stack is 1, 2, 4 with 4 on top. Note that + adds two Integers.
EXPLAIN: pop() returns 4, peek() then returns 2, and 4 + 2 = 6 (numeric addition, not string concatenation).

[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the value of the postfix expression 6 2 3 + * 4 - ?
A) 26
B) 32
C) 20
D) 14
ANSWER: A
HINT: Push numbers; when you see an operator pop two values, apply it, and push the result.
EXPLAIN: 2 + 3 = 5, 6 x 5 = 30, 30 - 4 = 26.

[MEDIUM]
Q: What does this Python code print?
CODE python
from collections import deque
q = deque([1, 2, 3])
q.append(4)
q.popleft()
q.appendleft(9)
print(list(q))
END
A) [9, 2, 3, 4]
B) [1, 2, 3, 4, 9]
C) [9, 1, 2, 3]
D) [2, 3, 4, 9]
ANSWER: A
HINT: popleft removes from the front; appendleft adds to the front.
EXPLAIN: [1,2,3] -> [1,2,3,4] -> [2,3,4] -> [9,2,3,4].

[HARD]
Q: Two stacks are used to implement a queue (push into stack IN, pop from stack OUT, refilling OUT from IN only when OUT is empty). What is the amortised cost of dequeue?
A) O(1)
B) O(log n)
C) O(n)
D) O(n^2)
ANSWER: A
HINT: Count how many times a single element can be moved between the two stacks over its lifetime.
EXPLAIN: Each element is pushed and popped at most twice in total, so n operations cost O(n): O(1) amortised.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] a = {2, 7, 3, 5, 4, 6, 8};
        int[] res = new int[a.length];
        Deque<Integer> st = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (int i = a.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            while (!st.isEmpty() && st.peek() <= a[i]) st.pop();
            res[i] = st.isEmpty() ? -1 : st.peek();
            st.push(a[i]);
        }
        for (int x : res) System.out.print(x + " ");
    }
}
END
A) 7 8 5 6 6 8 -1
B) 7 -1 5 6 6 8 -1
C) 7 8 5 6 6 8 8
D) -1 8 5 6 6 8 7
ANSWER: A
HINT: This is the "next greater element" pattern using a monotonic stack, scanned from the right.
EXPLAIN: For each element the stack keeps only larger values to its right; e.g. after 7 the next larger is 8, after 5 it is 6.

