[MEDIUM]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] edges = {{0, 1}, {0, 2}, {1, 3}, {2, 3}, {3, 4}};
        List<List<Integer>> g = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) g.add(new ArrayList<>());
        for (int[] e : edges) { g.get(e[0]).add(e[1]); g.get(e[1]).add(e[0]); }
        boolean[] seen = new boolean[5];
        Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.add(0); seen[0] = true;
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            int u = q.poll();
            System.out.print(u + " ");
            for (int v : g.get(u)) if (!seen[v]) { seen[v] = true; q.add(v); }
        }
    }
}
END
A) 0 1 2 3 4
B) 0 1 3 4 2
C) 0 2 3 1 4
D) 0 1 3 2 4
ANSWER: A
HINT: BFS finishes all neighbours of a vertex before going deeper.
EXPLAIN: 0 adds 1 and 2; 1 adds 3; 2 sees 3 already seen; 3 adds 4.

[MEDIUM]
Q: In an undirected graph, the sum of the degrees of all vertices equals:
A) The number of edges
B) Twice the number of edges
C) The number of vertices
D) V x E
ANSWER: B
HINT: Count how many endpoints each edge contributes.
EXPLAIN: Every edge adds 1 to the degree of each of its two endpoints (handshake lemma).

[MEDIUM]
Q: Which of these is a valid topological order of the DAG with edges A->B, A->C, B->D, C->D?
A) A C B D
B) B A C D
C) A D B C
D) D C B A
ANSWER: A
HINT: Every vertex must appear before all vertices it has an edge to.
EXPLAIN: A must come first and D last; B and C may appear in either order, so A C B D is valid.

[HARD]
Q: Dijkstra's algorithm may give WRONG shortest paths when:
A) The graph is directed
B) The graph contains negative edge weights
C) The graph has cycles
D) The graph is disconnected
ANSWER: B
HINT: Dijkstra finalises a vertex as soon as it is removed from the priority queue.
EXPLAIN: A negative edge found later could make an already-finalised distance shorter; Bellman-Ford handles negative edges.

[HARD]
Q: What is the output of this program?
CODE java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        final int INF = 1000000;
        int[][] d = {
            {0, 4, INF, 10},
            {INF, 0, 3, INF},
            {INF, INF, 0, 2},
            {INF, INF, INF, 0}
        };
        int n = 4;
        for (int k = 0; k < n; k++)
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
                for (int j = 0; j < n; j++)
                    if (d[i][k] + d[k][j] < d[i][j]) d[i][j] = d[i][k] + d[k][j];
        System.out.println(d[0][3] + " " + d[1][3]);
    }
}
END
A) 9 5
B) 10 5
C) 9 2
D) 6 5
ANSWER: A
HINT: This is Floyd-Warshall. Compare the direct edge 0->3 with the path 0->1->2->3.
EXPLAIN: 0->1->2->3 costs 4 + 3 + 2 = 9, cheaper than 10; 1->2->3 costs 3 + 2 = 5.

